mitting a...\r\n<!-- Check one of the following options with \"x\" -->\r\n<pre><code>\r\n[ ] Regression (a behavior that used to work and stopped working in a new release)\r\n[ ] Bug report  <!-- Please search GitHub for a similar issue or PR before submitting -->\r\n[ ] Performance issue\r\n[ ] Feature request\r\n[X] Documentation issue or request\r\n[ ] Support request => Please do not submit support request here, instead see https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#question\r\n[ ] Other... Please describe:\r\n</code></pre>\r\n\r\n## Current behavior\r\nIn the URL: https://angular.io/guide/architecture-components\r\nat the place where the \"class HeroListComponent\"  is defined, the explanation for the semantics of passing the argument \"private service: HeroService\" to the constructor method is missing.\r\nSomeone coming from the OOP background, they think that it is just a formal parameter and expects it to be initialized with the instance of the object created with new operator. \r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\nLooks like, in Angular, by virtue of specifying it as a formal parameter to constructor method it automatically does the following:\r\nprivate service: HeroService;\r\nconstructor() {\r\n   service = new HeroService();\r\n}\r\nIf this is the case, in case some parameters to be passed while constructing as HeroService(p1,p2,p3), how it is done when it can be done in the constructor() syntax.\r\n\r\n## Minimal reproduction of the problem with instructions\r\nNone\r\n\r\n## What is the motivation / use case for changing the behavior?\r\nNot Applicable\r\n\r\n\r\n## Environment\r\n\r\n<pre><code>\r\nAngular version: X.Y.Z\r\n<!-- Check whether this is still an issue in the most recent Angular version -->\r\n\r\nBrowser:  Not Applicable\r\n- [ ] Chrome (desktop) version XX\r\n- [ ] Chrome (Android) version XX\r\n- [ ] Chrome (iOS) version XX\r\n- [ ] Firefox version XX\r\n- [ ] Safari (desktop) version XX\r\n- [ ] Safari (iOS) version XX